// @test: typecheck-pass
// @tier: 0
// @level: L3
// @tags: types, generics, associated-types, iterator, T0997
// @description: One protocol method, one implementor, two element types — the second call must not be measured against the first call's type

// RED until T0997. Measured 2026-08-31: `expected 'Int', found 'Text'`
// at the SECOND call's argument, naming the FIRST call's element type.
//
// This is the minimised form, and every removed part is a refuted
// suspicion: `.collect()` is gone (fails without it), the two calls now
// use the SAME constructor (fails with one constructor), and the return
// annotations are gone (fails without them). What remains is the whole
// requirement: TWO calls, the SAME implementor, DIFFERENT element types.
//
// Each of those three matters. Two calls at the SAME element type pass.
// Two `let`s from the constructor at different element types, with no
// method call, pass. The same two calls split across two implementors
// (`MappedIter` and `Cloned`) pass.
//
// Measured NOT to be the cause, each with an instrument rather than a
// reading: the registered scheme is fully generalised (deep enumeration
// of the signature, not the shallow collector that builds the
// quantifier); it IS instantiated per call site, with fresh vars, and
// both sites receive the same correct signature
// `fn(StatefulTransducer<Item<MappedIter<_, _>>, _, _>) -> …`; the
// projection resolves separately for each receiver under distinct type
// keys; and the protocol impl is not mutated by matching.

mount core.base.iterator.{StatefulTransducer, Transducer};
fn first_int() {
    let data = [1, 1, 2];
    let xform: StatefulTransducer<Int, Int, Maybe<Int>> = Transducer.dedupe();
    let r = data.iter().map(|x| *x).transduce_stateful(xform);
}
fn then_text() {
    let data = ["a", "b", "c"];
    let xform: StatefulTransducer<Text, Text, Maybe<Text>> = Transducer.dedupe();
    let r = data.iter().map(|x| *x).transduce_stateful(xform);
}
fn main() { first_int(); then_text(); }
